</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80aa-8fa4-c349fc69e278" class="">I actually got help from this chat to do the codes: <a href="https://gemini.google.com/share/e1600f918b41">https://gemini.google.com/share/e1600f918b41</a></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ba-af13-c8533cfca284" class=""><strong>Section 3.2: Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)</strong></h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-806e-955c-ed370664a398" class=""><strong>1. Survival Rate by Sex</strong><br/>The analysis shows a massive disparity between male and female survival rates.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80f5-b254-e64055b8f21a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Females:</strong> ~74.2% survived.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8043-918b-f75510974c9d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Males:</strong> ~18.9% survived.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80a6-90d6-d2afc6ffc17e" class=""><em>Table for Report:</em><br/>| Sex | Survival Rate (%) | Total Passengers |<br/>| :--- | :--- | :--- |<br/>| <strong>Female</strong> | <strong>74.20%</strong> | 314 |<br/>| <strong>Male</strong> | 18.89% | 577 |</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8064-b843-f6bd3c369cfc" class=""><strong>2. Survival Rate by Ticket Class (Pclass)</strong><br/>There is a clear correlation between socio-economic status and survival. First-class passengers had the highest chance of survival, while third-class passengers had the lowest.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-804f-a435-f427cb8ebdb1" class=""><em>Table for Report:</em><br/>| Ticket Class | Survival Rate (%) | Total Passengers |<br/>| :--- | :--- | :--- |<br/>| <strong>1st Class</strong> | <strong>62.96%</strong> | 216 |<br/>| <strong>2nd Class</strong> | 47.28% | 184 |<br/>| <strong>3rd Class</strong> | 24.24% | 491 |</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80cc-97ea-e962d8ec37e0" class=""><strong>3. Average Age Analysis</strong><br/>The average age of survivors is slightly lower than non-survivors. This is likely due to the &quot;women and children first&quot; policy, where children (who have a low age) were prioritized, lowering the average age of the survivor group.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80b5-8978-e7bbae2f139b" class=""><em>Table for Report:</em><br/>| Group | Average Age | Count |<br/>| :--- | :--- | :--- |<br/>| <strong>Survivors (1)</strong> | 28.34 Years | 290 |<br/>| <strong>Non-Survivors (0)</strong> | 30.63 Years | 424 |</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8085-bd07-ecb6447116b7"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8011-a743-ffe23b049aaf" class=""><strong>Section 3.3 &amp; 3.4: Classification Model Results</strong></h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-802b-9d5d-d04f5c18a94c" class=""><strong>Model Configuration:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80c1-bba6-e27aca8ae6c4" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Algorithm:</strong> Logistic Regression</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80b5-a121-ea56d64d1218" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Features Used:</strong> Pclass, Sex (Indexed), Age, Fare, Embarked (Indexed)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80fb-9c8f-ea2bedf7aeda" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Data Split:</strong> 80% Training (598 records), 20% Testing (114 records)</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-803a-a71f-c9792e1663d6" class=""><strong>Model Performance:</strong><br/>The model achieved an accuracy of <strong>78.07%</strong> on the unseen test data. This indicates that the chosen features are strong predictors of survival.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80a7-8dae-c6ef1326aca0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Accuracy:</strong> <code>0.7807</code></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8011-8b6e-d3d0b6c6d617" class=""><strong>HDFS File Storage Verification:</strong><br/>The accuracy result was successfully saved to a text file and uploaded to the HDFS storage as required.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80fa-bf69-c2b927e72ead" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>File Path:</strong> <code>/karimi/accuracy.txt</code></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-801f-ab4c-f4b0d011a7f7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Content:</strong> <code>Accuracy: 0.7807</code></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8075-8fb5-c537c42a0c1f" class=""><strong>Proof of Upload (Screenshot/Command Log):</strong><em>(You can copy this part of your terminal output into the report as proof)</em></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-806d-a4b1-ed937535f47e" class="">This is the compact code for this part:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8089-a0b1-ec96613b0075" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">import os
from pyspark.sql import SparkSession
from pyspark.sql.functions import col, avg, count
from pyspark.ml.feature import StringIndexer, VectorAssembler
from pyspark.ml.classification import LogisticRegression
from pyspark.ml.evaluation import MulticlassClassificationEvaluator

# ==========================================
# 1. SETUP &amp; CONFIGURATION
# ==========================================
APP_NAME = &quot;Titanic_Analysis_Karimi&quot;
HDFS_PATH = &quot;hdfs://172.18.32.200:9000/karimi/Titanic-Dataset.csv&quot;
HDFS_OUTPUT_DIR = &quot;/karimi/accuracy.txt&quot;
LOCAL_FILENAME = &quot;accuracy.txt&quot;

# Initialize Spark Session
spark = SparkSession.builder \
    .appName(APP_NAME) \
    .getOrCreate()

spark.sparkContext.setLogLevel(&quot;ERROR&quot;)  # Reduce log noise

# ==========================================
# 2. LOAD DATA
# ==========================================
print(f&quot;\n[INFO] Loading data from: {HDFS_PATH} ...&quot;)
try:
    df = spark.read.csv(HDFS_PATH, header=True, inferSchema=True)
except Exception as e:
    print(f&quot;[ERROR] Could not load file. Make sure HDFS path is correct. Error: {e}&quot;)
    spark.stop()
    exit(1)

# ==========================================
# 3. EXPLORATORY DATA ANALYSIS (EDA)
# ==========================================
print(&quot;\n&quot; + &quot;=&quot;*40)
print(&quot; PART 3.2: EXPLORATORY DATA ANALYSIS&quot;)
print(&quot;=&quot;*40)

# 3.1 Survival Rate by Sex
print(&quot;\n--- Survival Rate by Sex ---&quot;)
sex_survival = df.groupBy(&quot;Sex&quot;).agg(
    (avg(&quot;Survived&quot;) * 100).alias(&quot;Survival_Rate_Percent&quot;),
    count(&quot;PassengerId&quot;).alias(&quot;Total_Passengers&quot;)
)
sex_survival.show()

# 3.2 Survival Rate by Pclass
print(&quot;\n--- Survival Rate by Ticket Class (Pclass) ---&quot;)
class_survival = df.groupBy(&quot;Pclass&quot;).agg(
    (avg(&quot;Survived&quot;) * 100).alias(&quot;Survival_Rate_Percent&quot;),
    count(&quot;PassengerId&quot;).alias(&quot;Total_Passengers&quot;)
).orderBy(&quot;Pclass&quot;)
class_survival.show()

# 3.3 Average Age Statistics
print(&quot;\n--- Average Age: Survivors vs Non-Survivors ---&quot;)
age_analysis = df.groupBy(&quot;Survived&quot;).agg(
    avg(&quot;Age&quot;).alias(&quot;Average_Age&quot;),
    count(&quot;Age&quot;).alias(&quot;Count_With_Age_Data&quot;)
)
age_analysis.show()

# ==========================================
# 4. MACHINE LEARNING PIPELINE (PART 3.3)
# ==========================================
print(&quot;\n&quot; + &quot;=&quot;*40)
print(&quot; PART 3.3: MACHINE LEARNING PIPELINE&quot;)
print(&quot;=&quot;*40)

# 4.1 Data Preparation
# Select features and drop missing values
required_columns = [&quot;Pclass&quot;, &quot;Sex&quot;, &quot;Age&quot;, &quot;Fare&quot;, &quot;Embarked&quot;, &quot;Survived&quot;]
data = df.select(required_columns).dropna()

# 4.2 Feature Engineering (StringIndexer)
# Convert &#x27;Sex&#x27; and &#x27;Embarked&#x27; to numerical indices
sex_indexer = StringIndexer(inputCol=&quot;Sex&quot;, outputCol=&quot;SexIndex&quot;)
data = sex_indexer.fit(data).transform(data)

embarked_indexer = StringIndexer(inputCol=&quot;Embarked&quot;, outputCol=&quot;EmbarkedIndex&quot;)
data = embarked_indexer.fit(data).transform(data)

# 4.3 Vector Assembly
feature_cols = [&quot;Pclass&quot;, &quot;SexIndex&quot;, &quot;Age&quot;, &quot;Fare&quot;, &quot;EmbarkedIndex&quot;]
assembler = VectorAssembler(inputCols=feature_cols, outputCol=&quot;features&quot;)
final_data = assembler.transform(data)

# 4.4 Split Data
train_data, test_data = final_data.randomSplit([0.8, 0.2], seed=42)
print(f&quot;Training Dataset Count: {train_data.count()}&quot;)
print(f&quot;Test Dataset Count:     {test_data.count()}&quot;)

# 4.5 Train Model
print(&quot;\n[INFO] Training Logistic Regression Model...&quot;)
lr = LogisticRegression(featuresCol=&quot;features&quot;, labelCol=&quot;Survived&quot;)
lr_model = lr.fit(train_data)

# 4.6 Make Predictions
predictions = lr_model.transform(test_data)

# 4.7 Evaluate
evaluator = MulticlassClassificationEvaluator(
    labelCol=&quot;Survived&quot;, predictionCol=&quot;prediction&quot;, metricName=&quot;accuracy&quot;
)
accuracy = evaluator.evaluate(predictions)
print(f&quot;\n&gt;&gt;&gt; Model Accuracy on Test Data: {accuracy:.4f}&quot;)

# ==========================================
# 5. SAVE RESULTS (PART 3.4)
# ==========================================
print(&quot;\n&quot; + &quot;=&quot;*40)
print(&quot; PART 3.4: SAVING RESULTS TO HDFS&quot;)
print(&quot;=&quot;*40)

# Step A: Write to local file
with open(LOCAL_FILENAME, &quot;w&quot;) as f:
    f.write(f&quot;Accuracy: {accuracy:.4f}&quot;)
print(f&quot;[INFO] Accuracy saved locally to: {LOCAL_FILENAME}&quot;)

# Step B: Upload to HDFS
# Using -f flag to overwrite if exists
upload_cmd = f&quot;hdfs dfs -put -f {LOCAL_FILENAME} {HDFS_OUTPUT_DIR}&quot;
print(f&quot;[INFO] Executing: {upload_cmd}&quot;)

exit_code = os.system(upload_cmd)

if exit_code == 0:
    print(&quot;[SUCCESS] Successfully uploaded accuracy.txt to HDFS!&quot;)
else:
    print(&quot;[ERROR] Failed to upload to HDFS. Check permissions or path.&quot;)

# Stop Spark Session
spark.stop()</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h1 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80d5-92b9-e5bd1347554c" class="">Explanation of the code:</h1></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8043-a7f0-cf21229d0540" class="">1</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-801d-a130-cf2661d5ea7a" class="">Based on the assignment and our previous steps, <strong>Question 3.1</strong> was not about Python code. It was about using <strong>Shell (Terminal) Commands</strong> to interact with the Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS).</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80e4-8e3e-c7f70c014bc1" class="">Here is the explanation of what you did and the commands you used for Question 3.1. You should include these commands in your report.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-801a-8734-d505d58ef866" class="">The Concept</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-804f-869a-e289bb48bb94" class="">Before Spark (the Python code) can analyze big data, that data must live inside the distributed storage system (HDFS), not just on one local hard drive. Question 3.1 asks you to create a specific folder in that storage system and upload your file there.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80c2-9c46-fb562bccbcf9" class="">The Commands Explained</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8002-ace1-dd8d3cc075c2" class="">1. Creating the Directory</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8075-88db-e964611c7e19" class=""><strong>Command:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8056-885a-fd67c02bce88" class="">Bash</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80df-a8ca-d6dc4861673a" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /karimi</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-803c-9528-cab799a93170" class=""><strong>Explanation:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8073-a3b9-ca7763e5ec58" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code>hdfs</code>: This calls the Hadoop software.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-800d-a960-fd0ede294d1c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code>dfs</code>: Tells Hadoop we want to do a <strong>Distributed File System</strong> operation.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-804e-8535-f3b615345187" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code>mkdir</code>: Short for &quot;Make Directory&quot; (create a folder).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-804b-bcc3-e8bae3dab98a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code>p</code>: This flag is very useful. It means &quot;no error if existing&quot; (if the folder already exists, don&#x27;t crash) and &quot;create parents&quot; (create any necessary sub-folders automatically).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8027-b1ec-c1c80cd903e2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code>/karimi</code>: This is the folder name you chose to create inside HDFS.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ec-8aaf-c7b28c866eda" class="">2. Uploading the Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-801d-96f5-f0cd11925a1b" class=""><strong>Command:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80f8-a0f3-cf6fc7ff9075" class="">Bash</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8092-b0c1-ed9515727f0f" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">hdfs dfs -put Titanic-Dataset.csv /karimi/</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80aa-a7fa-df08d1601439" class=""><strong>Explanation:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80db-9930-dd873556ba61" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code>put</code>: This is the command to <strong>copy</strong> a file from your <strong>Local Linux Filesystem</strong> (where you are logged in via SSH) into the <strong>HDFS</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80da-a48a-e583e19c9b97" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code>Titanic-Dataset.csv</code>: The source file on your local machine.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8014-9334-ebd0efa8d80d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code>/karimi/</code>: The destination folder inside the HDFS.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80c8-a8cc-e396447fad55" class="">3. Verifying the Upload</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80bd-8f36-e4dbe3c6e72a" class=""><strong>Command:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8015-a895-c6463141a3d4" class="">Bash</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ba-a65c-e3671d4305d7" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">hdfs dfs -ls /karimi/</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80d0-95eb-ebf7d6005dbb" class=""><strong>Explanation:</strong></p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80c1-bc61-d92ccabc308b" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code>ls</code>: Short for &quot;List&quot;. It shows the contents of a directory.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8005-a753-d56096dd29f2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Why we do this: To confirm the file actually arrived. In your terminal output, you saw:<div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80dc-8f20-fcb05ab91aa8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">rw-r--r-- 1 karimi dmls 61194 ... /karimi/Titanic-Dataset.csv</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8073-a204-defe4d98131a" class="">This confirms the file size (61KB) and location are correct.</p></div></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80fb-8a25-de94aef77db7" class="">Summary for your Report</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8095-8e9e-ea089ce770b9" class="">For Question 3.1 in your report, you should write:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ab-8911-fc5dc61f7a5f" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Objective:</strong> To prepare the HDFS environment for analysis.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-806b-8a8d-e25ff223b3ed" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Created a directory using <code>hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /karimi</code>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8069-9d8d-ffce7899501d" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Uploaded the dataset using <code>hdfs dfs -put Titanic-Dataset.csv /karimi/</code>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-800d-9790-fc1cf0494e1e" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Verified the upload using <code>hdfs dfs -ls /karimi/</code>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-809e-ac5c-c19d59853b37" class="">(This step was mandatory because the Python script in 3.2 connects to <code>hdfs://.../karimi/Titanic-Dataset.csv</code> to read this file).</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80d6-8f5e-e8352c99874c"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ed-8740-ec31a8f648c9" class="">2</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80b9-b3c4-fb9e9d264d4f" class="">Here is the detailed explanation of the Python code for <strong>Part 3.2 (Exploratory Data Analysis)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8057-aef3-dd00f4b14c64" class="">This section of the code uses <strong>PySpark SQL</strong> to perform statistical analysis on the distributed data. Because the data is in HDFS, Spark distributes the calculation across the cluster nodes (though for this small dataset, it likely runs fast on one node).</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80f1-ab4e-c8411abec8ea" class="">1. Survival Rate by Sex</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80da-8b64-c760138dd830" class=""><strong>The Goal:</strong> Find out what percentage of men vs. women survived.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80fb-a2c5-e35bd432df62" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-800c-bf56-de35c4fabb9d" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">sex_survival = df.groupBy(&quot;Sex&quot;).agg(
    (avg(&quot;Survived&quot;) * 100).alias(&quot;Survival_Rate_Percent&quot;),
    count(&quot;PassengerId&quot;).alias(&quot;Total_Passengers&quot;)
)
sex_survival.show()</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8028-a47e-f379e0357c5c" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>df.groupBy(&quot;Sex&quot;)</strong></code>: This splits the data into two groups: &quot;male&quot; rows and &quot;female&quot; rows.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80a9-8870-c5c3f927811a" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>.agg(...)</strong></code>: Short for <strong>Aggregate</strong>. It allows us to perform calculations on each group.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8029-8c7e-d8ec3930b2cc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>avg(&quot;Survived&quot;)</strong></code>:<div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8005-8241-ddfb81aa31cf" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">In the dataset, <code>Survived</code> is <strong>0</strong> (No) or <strong>1</strong> (Yes).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80e8-989c-e4c9011027b2" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">The mathematical average of a 0/1 column represents the <strong>probability</strong> or <strong>rate</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8013-a62c-e76759a627b0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle"><em>Example:</em> If you have [1, 1, 0, 0], the sum is 2, count is 4. Average = 0.5 (50%).</li></ul></div></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-800d-992c-c2a4233dad9d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>100</strong></code>: Converts the decimal (e.g., 0.74) into a percentage (74.0).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ea-92fc-db5507242289" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>.alias(...)</strong></code>: Renames the ugly default column name (like <code>(avg(Survived) * 100)</code>) to something readable like <code>&quot;Survival_Rate_Percent&quot;</code>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8009-a0cc-dc1164941381" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>.show()</strong></code>: This corresponds to an <strong>Action</strong> in Spark. It triggers the calculation and prints the result table to your terminal.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80fb-a67e-fdd7be47d072"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8088-9e77-ccf68ea4108e" class="">2. Survival Rate by Ticket Class (Pclass)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8038-9cfc-e795858bc03c" class=""><strong>The Goal:</strong> See if rich passengers (Class 1) survived more than poor passengers (Class 3).</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8038-8eda-d0bab84c62c8" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80b0-823d-ce3ebdaaf7fc" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">class_survival = df.groupBy(&quot;Pclass&quot;).agg(
    (avg(&quot;Survived&quot;) * 100).alias(&quot;Survival_Rate_Percent&quot;),
    count(&quot;PassengerId&quot;).alias(&quot;Total_Passengers&quot;)
).orderBy(&quot;Pclass&quot;)
class_survival.show()</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80d2-a850-e79c6a167705" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>groupBy(&quot;Pclass&quot;)</strong></code>: Splits data into groups: 1, 2, and 3.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8070-9f52-eddaff5664b7" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Logic</strong>: Same math as above (Average of 0s and 1s).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80d1-8418-d42b52454858" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>.orderBy(&quot;Pclass&quot;)</strong></code>: This sorts the final table. Without this, the rows might appear in random order (e.g., 2, 1, 3). We want them sorted 1 $\to$ 2 $\to$ 3.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8050-9568-fb4dc52b6b68"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80bb-834c-fea73465f569" class="">3. Average Age: Survivors vs. Non-Survivors</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-802f-a9c8-db3cf1fff7aa" class=""><strong>The Goal:</strong> Check if age played a role. (Did children survive more? Did older people die?)</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-809d-b58b-c4d1d5f4af0b" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ec-969a-e887a520de14" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">age_analysis = df.groupBy(&quot;Survived&quot;).agg(
    avg(&quot;Age&quot;).alias(&quot;Average_Age&quot;),
    count(&quot;Age&quot;).alias(&quot;Count_With_Age_Data&quot;)
)
age_analysis.show()</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80e5-8e17-d8ed82451521" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>groupBy(&quot;Survived&quot;)</strong></code>:<div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8037-8cf6-c62876824a13" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">Group <strong>0</strong>: People who died.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80e1-8aad-c2cde28df297" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">Group <strong>1</strong>: People who survived.</li></ul></div></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8070-acbd-e2155bd09489" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>avg(&quot;Age&quot;)</strong></code>: Calculates the average age for each group.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-801f-825b-fa9b36fe09f0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>count(&quot;Age&quot;)</strong></code>: Counts how many people in that group actually had an Age listed (some rows in Titanic have <code>null</code> for Age).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8074-aa6d-c57af34d593f" class="">Summary for Report (Part 3.2)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8065-b744-feb2f1f7431c" class="">When writing your report for 3.2, you can explain that you used <strong>Spark SQL Aggregations</strong> to derive these insights.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8069-8069-cce8f5b20281" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Insight 1:</strong> Gender was a massive factor (Females ~74% survival vs Males ~18%).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8018-b772-f7971b8c88f6" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Insight 2:</strong> Class was a massive factor (1st Class ~62% vs 3rd Class ~24%).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80c9-917f-c636b6ed2203" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Insight 3:</strong> Age had a smaller impact, but survivors were slightly younger on average (likely due to children being saved first).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="2de3bd7d-3b19-806b-860d-edfcf8cf63c0"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8041-9176-f872ab5d7b82" class="">3</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8034-98bf-e9967cd3d168" class="">Here is the detailed explanation of the code for <strong>Part 3.3 (Machine Learning Pipeline)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8003-b92b-db2016a691b0" class="">This part represents the core &quot;Artificial Intelligence&quot; task. You are training a computer algorithm to look at passenger details (Age, Class, Sex, etc.) and guess whether they survived or not.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80d2-81b2-f8273e7247d2" class="">Step 1: Data Preparation</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-803b-9223-da76ca7b196d" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-806f-9597-db0b137cf087" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">required_columns = [&quot;Pclass&quot;, &quot;Sex&quot;, &quot;Age&quot;, &quot;Fare&quot;, &quot;Embarked&quot;, &quot;Survived&quot;]
data = df.select(required_columns).dropna()</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80d8-8df1-cb80def7f7ae" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>Selection:</strong> We filter the huge dataset down to only the columns that matter for prediction. Passenger Name or Ticket ID usually don&#x27;t help predict survival, so we ignore them.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-805a-b408-ff5e90f46618" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>dropna()</strong></code>: Machine Learning algorithms generally <strong>crash</strong> if you feed them empty data (Nulls). This command deletes any row that has a missing value in these columns.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-800a-8ea0-cd6bef8b2a58" class="">Step 2: Feature Engineering (StringIndexer)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80a1-af9f-e0ec306f567f" class="">Computers cannot do math with words like &quot;male&quot; or &quot;female&quot;. They only understand numbers.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-800a-8d86-e8acc80a2efe" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80f2-8d8c-ee87e2d9d9dd" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">sex_indexer = StringIndexer(inputCol=&quot;Sex&quot;, outputCol=&quot;SexIndex&quot;)
data = sex_indexer.fit(data).transform(data)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80e0-ad9c-edfe61f9f09e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>StringIndexer</strong></code>: This is a tool that translates words into numbers.<div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80d8-9ea4-eb6292918edf" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">&quot;Male&quot; $\rightarrow$ 0</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80f4-b6f9-ea63a347c1e0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:circle">&quot;Female&quot; $\rightarrow$ 1</li></ul></div></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8018-9276-eadd8012cc90" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">We do this for <code>Sex</code> and <code>Embarked</code> (where the ship stopped: S, C, or Q). The result is stored in new columns called <code>SexIndex</code> and <code>EmbarkedIndex</code>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80b8-bfb6-c5f740c7fec7" class="">Step 3: Vector Assembly (Crucial Spark Concept)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-807f-910f-eedf1b4a87c7" class="">This is unique to Spark ML and very important to understand.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8002-b25a-f2a64181126f" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8020-8aeb-daff2934d4b8" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">feature_cols = [&quot;Pclass&quot;, &quot;SexIndex&quot;, &quot;Age&quot;, &quot;Fare&quot;, &quot;EmbarkedIndex&quot;]
assembler = VectorAssembler(inputCols=feature_cols, outputCol=&quot;features&quot;)
final_data = assembler.transform(data)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-805f-a3b1-ca0754bbfbd8" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Most Python libraries (like Scikit-Learn) let you feed multiple columns into a model. <strong>Spark does not.</strong></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8030-ab46-c1c591909edb" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">Spark requires <strong>ALL</strong> your input numbers to be combined into a <strong>single list</strong> (a vector) inside <strong>one column</strong>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8076-b990-fc7754f98120" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>VectorAssembler</strong></code>: It takes the 5 columns (<code>Pclass</code>, <code>Age</code>, etc.) and squashes them into one column named <code>&quot;features&quot;</code>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8013-9d62-dd68f11a4005" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><em>Example row:</em> Instead of <code>Age: 22, Sex: 0, Class: 3</code>, Spark sees <code>features: [3.0, 0.0, 22.0, ...]</code>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8054-989c-cf8f79e97be0" class="">Step 4: Splitting the Data</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-801a-bf91-d2a891b994e1" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8082-b6ea-f004873d81c0" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">train_data, test_data = final_data.randomSplit([0.8, 0.2], seed=42)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-806c-8196-ea27280910be" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">We cannot test the model on the same data it studied (that would be cheating).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80e0-903f-e8e5659e3f2e" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>80% (Train):</strong> Used for the model to learn patterns.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-805d-8c7d-d438accb6406" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><strong>20% (Test):</strong> Used to exam the model later to see how good it is.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80dd-9bde-dda787c701fd" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>seed=42</strong></code>: Ensures the &quot;random&quot; split is exactly the same every time you run the code (reproducibility).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8083-a91f-c947d621132f" class="">Step 5: Training the Model</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8018-b12e-fc965c94d833" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8007-ba00-c93f453f0a27" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">lr = LogisticRegression(featuresCol=&quot;features&quot;, labelCol=&quot;Survived&quot;)
lr_model = lr.fit(train_data)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80d6-9d46-f3a9e6cd0253" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>LogisticRegression</strong></code>: This is the chosen algorithm. It works well for &quot;Yes/No&quot; questions (Binary Classification).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-802c-b27d-d4a7a2752747" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>featuresCol</strong></code>: We tell it where the input data is (the vector we made in Step 3).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-807c-a6ae-dbcc036cd1ac" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>labelCol</strong></code>: We tell it what the correct answer is (<code>Survived</code>).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-808b-a3f9-da2a2a62bee0" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>.fit(train_data)</strong></code>: This is the command that starts the learning process. The computer iterates over the 598 training rows to find mathematical patterns.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80a1-b174-c7b9142fd0ab" class="">Step 6: Making Predictions</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80b1-8255-ea7daa045d2e" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8049-80f7-dc10a87b3da7" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">predictions = lr_model.transform(test_data)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80e1-b3db-e520aba71cef" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>.transform(test_data)</strong></code>: Now that the model is trained, we show it the <strong>Test Data</strong> (the 20% it has never seen).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-801c-8166-e95314028adc" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc">It looks at the features and makes a guess, storing it in a new column called <code>prediction</code>.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80bf-ba51-ecde1098bb1e" class="">Summary for Report</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-807d-8fbb-fadf7e2e27de" class="">For Part 3.3, you are building a pipeline that:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8028-93cf-d51ce90ba775" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li>Cleans the data (removes Nulls).</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8078-8626-e62f5af035d5" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li>Translates text to numbers (<code>StringIndexer</code>).</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80cd-87e8-c06f591264aa" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li>Combines features into a single vector (<code>VectorAssembler</code>).</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ac-b33f-f7febec5f72c" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li>Trains a Logistic Regression model on 80% of the data.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><hr id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80c4-b679-f9473376d660"/></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h2 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8024-8e99-f89e2ad74204" class="">4</h2></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8024-8564-e8e7794240ff" class="">Here is the detailed explanation for <strong>Part 3.4 (Saving Results)</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80b2-9be3-e64b584f2fb4" class="">This part is crucial because in a real distributed system, the computer running the code (the &quot;Driver&quot;) is often temporary. If you don&#x27;t save your results to the permanent storage (HDFS), you might lose them when the program finishes.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-807f-939d-de620be49a78" class="">The Code Logic</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8055-8390-df08cd5b023e" class="">Step 1: Write to Local Disk First</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80f9-9a3b-c21b7fce65c5" class="">Spark (Python) cannot write a simple text file directly to HDFS easily without using specific Hadoop libraries. The standard and easiest way is to write it to the <strong>local Linux file system</strong> first.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80a9-afe4-dfa84a092fd8" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ab-b025-d70ff676e23f" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">with open(LOCAL_FILENAME, &quot;w&quot;) as f:
    f.write(f&quot;Accuracy: {accuracy:.4f}&quot;)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80ee-85ed-d4279f0e3536" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>open(..., &quot;w&quot;)</strong></code>: Opens a file named <code>accuracy.txt</code> in <strong>Write</strong> mode on the computer you are currently using (<code>raspberrypi-dml0</code>).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8014-bc03-d015e5724559" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>f.write(...)</strong></code>: Writes the calculated accuracy (e.g., <code>0.7807</code>) inside that file.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80c0-a078-e437403ce01c" class="">Step 2: Upload from Local to HDFS</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8002-97d0-c07e0639bd21" class="">Now that the file exists locally, we need to move it to the distributed storage. Since we are inside a Python script, we need a way to run a <strong>Terminal Command</strong>.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8082-b79d-f2653ffcb005" class="">Python</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/prism.min.js" integrity="sha512-7Z9J3l1+EYfeaPKcGXu3MS/7T+w19WtKQY/n+xzmw4hZhJ9tyYmcUS+4QqAlzhicE5LAfMQSF3iFTK9bQdTxXg==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.29.0/themes/prism.min.css" integrity="sha512-tN7Ec6zAFaVSG3TpNAKtk4DOHNpSwKHxxrsiw4GHKESGPs5njn/0sMCUMl2svV4wo4BK/rCP7juYz+zx+l6oeQ==" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerPolicy="no-referrer"/><pre id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8016-beb3-e435cec2d3fb" class="code code-wrap"><code class="language-JavaScript" style="white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-all">import os
...
upload_cmd = f&quot;hdfs dfs -put -f {LOCAL_FILENAME} {HDFS_OUTPUT_DIR}&quot;
exit_code = os.system(upload_cmd)</code></pre></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80aa-84b9-f73818a1d61d" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>import os</strong></code>: This library allows Python to talk to the Operating System (Linux).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8074-ad11-f57d38869104" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>hdfs dfs -put</strong></code>: The standard Hadoop command to upload files.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8044-9613-fa2945794297" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>f</strong></code>: This stands for <strong>Force</strong>. It means <em>&quot;If the file already exists in HDFS, overwrite it.&quot;</em> This is very useful if you run your code multiple times (so it doesn&#x27;t crash on the second try).</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8053-9288-d055c049a0de" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>os.system(upload_cmd)</strong></code>: This tells Python: <em>&quot;Pause for a second, open the terminal, run this command, and then come back.&quot;</em></li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ul id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80c9-9e8d-ea9b6b9d9989" class="bulleted-list"><li style="list-style-type:disc"><code><strong>exit_code</strong></code>: If the command works, Linux returns <code>0</code>. If it fails (e.g., permission denied), it returns a non-zero number. We check this to print &quot;Success&quot; or &quot;Error&quot;.</li></ul></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><h3 id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80c0-aae4-e1a7c6f8376b" class="">Summary for Report (Part 3.4)</h3></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8051-bd42-f6077fc84100" class="">In your report for this section, you should state that:</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80cc-b911-d3f89e237721" class="numbered-list" start="1"><li><strong>Method:</strong> You calculated the accuracy using the <code>MulticlassClassificationEvaluator</code>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8073-8030-e0c4f0183bee" class="numbered-list" start="2"><li><strong>Persistence:</strong> You saved the result to a local file (<code>accuracy.txt</code>) and programmatically uploaded it to HDFS using <code>os.system</code>.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8028-b2a1-c96ff3583de3" class="numbered-list" start="3"><li><strong>Result Location:</strong> The final file is stored at <code>/karimi/accuracy.txt</code> in HDFS.</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><ol type="1" id="2de3bd7d-3b19-80aa-9595-ed4d56986666" class="numbered-list" start="4"><li><strong>Accuracy Value:</strong> You achieved an accuracy of <strong>0.7807</strong> (or 78.07%).</li></ol></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8074-afdc-f815f8f86cf5" class="">This completes the full explanation of the entire script! You are now fully prepared to write the report.</p></div><div style="display:contents" dir="auto"><p id="2de3bd7d-3b19-8069-a52c-df6ba8c7afcc" class="">
</p></div></div></article></body></html>